# PostgreSQL as the source of truth for money — the complete, ordered set.
#
#   Generated from backend/postgres/moneyAuthority.js (PATH_ENV, flip order).
#   Verify before use:  npm run preflight:flip
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THIS IS
#
# Authority is environment-only, by design: a flip is a deploy-time decision an
# operator makes deliberately and reverts by redeploying. This file is the exact
# set to paste into your deploy environment. Nothing in the repository reads it
# automatically, and that is deliberate — a committed file that silently moved
# the source of truth for money would be the "hiding incomplete work behind
# configuration" the governance rules forbid.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BEFORE YOU SET ANY OF THESE
#
#   npm run preflight:flip
#
# It needs DATABASE_URL and MONGODB_URI, and it answers one question with an
# exit code. It distinguishes two situations:
#
#   GREENFIELD   Nothing has launched; both stores are empty. There is no
#                migration. Set the whole block at once and launch with
#                Postgres authoritative from the first boot.
#
#   MIGRATION    MongoDB holds real money. Run
#                `npm run reconcile:pg -- --all --backfill` FIRST — it adopts
#                order_states, user_kyc, casino_transactions and bets, which no
#                forward mirror reaches. Then set ONE variable per deploy, in
#                the order below, watching the metrics between each.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT DOES **NOT** CHANGE WHEN YOU SET THESE
#
# MongoDB is still required and still running. Under Postgres authority:
#
#   - READS still come from Mongo — history, settlement sweeps, analytics,
#     merchant scoring, assignment eligibility. The authoritative check is the
#     transaction itself, which refuses transactionally, so a stale read can
#     misroute an order but never move money wrongly.
#   - The REVERSE MIRROR writes Mongo on every authoritative transaction, which
#     is what makes rolling back a redeploy rather than a data-loss event.
#
# Removing MongoDB is Phase C. It is deliberately not modelled anywhere in this
# repository — it is a data-retention decision plus a PITR restore drill, not a
# configuration change. Do not delete the Mongo wiring after flipping these.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ROLLING BACK
#
# Unset the variable and redeploy. Mongo has been kept current the whole time.
# Roll back in REVERSE order of the list below, so a path never outlives a
# dependency it reads from.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT GIVE YOU
#
# infrastructureTested is 0/11 and no configuration can change it. Restart
# Postgres under load, restart Mongo's primary, kill a backend mid-transaction,
# run two app instances — GO_LIVE_RUNBOOK.md §2.2. And nobody has yet booted
# this application against real services at all; the runbook's hand smoke-test
# is the single highest-value thing to do before any of this.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1. Balances and the wallet ledger. Everything else reads from these.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_WALLET=postgres

# 2. Merchant token balances. Depends on WALLET: a deposit confirmation debits
#    the merchant and credits the user inside ONE transaction.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_MERCHANT_WALLET=postgres

# 3. Double-entry accounting. Derived from completed movements, so it cannot be
#    authoritative while balances are still in Mongo.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_LEDGER=postgres

# 4. Order lifecycle. order_states + append-only order_transitions, with the
#    accounting event posted in the SAME transaction as the state change.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_ORDERS=postgres

# 5. KYC decisions. Cuts over after the money paths by design.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_KYC=postgres

# 6. User↔merchant settlement of deposits and withdrawals.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_MERCHANT_SETTLEMENT=postgres

# 7. Admin treasury and token issuance.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_ADMIN_ISSUANCE=postgres

# 8. Bet lifecycle and stake reservation — placement AND settlement.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_BETS=postgres

# 9. Cycle settlement and payout. Depends on BETS.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_SETTLEMENTS=postgres

# 10. Casino provider callbacks. Depends on WALLET and LEDGER, not on BETS —
#     the provider path calls the wallet directly, not the bet engine.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_CASINO_SETTLEMENT=postgres

# 11. Bonus engine and referral commission. NOTE the variable name does not
#     follow the path name ("bonuses_and_commissions"); this is the one most
#     often mistyped, which is why the list is generated rather than written.
MONEY_AUTHORITY_BONUSES=postgres
